# Python and local environments
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
*.tmp

# Credentials. Never commit these: see .env.example.
.env

# Research cache: daily prices, re-downloadable at any time.
research/cache/

# Deliberately NOT ignored, though the bot writes them:
#
#   orders_placed*.json    the order ledgers, one per account: orders_placed.json for
#                          Trading 212 live, orders_placed.demo.json for its demo,
#                          orders_placed.paper.json for the paper account, and
#                          orders_placed.<adapter>.json for any other adapter. Trading 212's
#                          API is not idempotent, so a ledger is written before each order
#                          and is what stops a repeat and counts the daily cap.
#   momentum_targets.json  this month's momentum book, built once a month.
#   paper_account.json     the paper account's cash, holdings and orders.
#
# GitHub Actions throws away its disk after every run, so a run there only remembers what
# the workflow commits back to the repository, and these files are that memory. Ignoring
# them would let the next run repeat an order or spend past the daily cap.
#
#   HALT                   the killswitch. A committed HALT is how to stop a bot that runs
#                          on GitHub's machines from a browser; ignoring it would make the
#                          killswitch unusable everywhere except a local computer.
#
# Those files carry your cash, holdings and every order, and so do the run summaries and
# issues the workflow writes. A repository that trades through GitHub Actions this way must
# be PRIVATE. On your own computer or in Docker you need not commit them at all.
